docs/program/charters/E26-vocabulary-kinds-the-records-lanes-asked-for-CODEX-REVIEW.log:424:schema/manifest.schema.json:5:  "description": "What a person selected from the catalogue and where its files are. Written by `osd manifest` (and, later, the site), read by `osd get` and `osd.manifest.read`. Assets are the STAC Collection assets, which are the files the verifier hashed: a sample, never the whole dataset unless `coverage` says complete. Never carries credentials. v2 (E07): an asset may name `alternates`, other locations of the same bytes (the mirror), tried before `href`; a manifest with alternates says osd.manifest/2, one without may stay osd.manifest/1. v3 (E09): `metadata.catalog` names the catalogue snapshot the manifest was written from (records.json `catalog`, copied whole); a /3 manifest carries it, /1 and /2 must not. A /3 licence may state `attribution: null` (the source states no value), which /1 and /2 refuse. v4 (E19): the record/3 vocabulary travels: an entry may say `access: purchase` and its licence may carry `terms`, copied whole (absent keys stay absent); a /4 manifest carries `metadata.catalog` like /3, and /1 to /3 refuse both new values, so an old manifest means what it meant. A manifest is written as /4 only when an entry needs it.",
docs/program/charters/E26-vocabulary-kinds-the-records-lanes-asked-for-CODEX-REVIEW.log:432:schema/manifest.schema.json:79:                  "description": "v4. The record's licence.terms (record/3), copied whole: the keys the record states, nothing added",
